\description{
Draws a legend for a column colored with \code{\link[=gt_color_ranks]{gt_color_ranks()}} or
\code{gt::data_color()}. Pass it the same \code{columns} and \code{palette} you colored with
and the legend will match the table, including the domain it takes from the
data. For a hand-built key of labeled swatches, use \code{\link[=gt_legend_discrete]{gt_legend_discrete()}}.
}
\details{
The bar can be a smooth gradient, a stepped ramp, or separated blocks. The
title can sit on any side of it, and the title and bound labels are styled
independently through \code{title_style} and \code{labels_style}.

The bar is drawn as a series of solid color segments instead of a CSS
gradient. \code{gt}'s inline-CSS path, used by the RStudio Viewer, Quarto and R
Markdown, strips gradient backgrounds, and the legend would disappear. Segment
colors come from \code{scales::col_numeric}, the same function \code{gt::data_color()}
uses, so the ramp matches the cells exactly.

\code{\link[=gt_title_header]{gt_title_header()}} calls \code{gt::tab_header()}, which replaces the whole header.
With \code{location = "top"}, call \code{\link[=gt_title_header]{gt_title_header()}} first or it will overwrite
the legend.
}
\section{Styling}{


\code{title_style} and \code{labels_style} are named lists following the same convention
as \code{\link[=gt_grid]{gt_grid()}} and \code{\link[=gt_title_header]{gt_title_header()}}. Recognized keys are \code{font} (a Google
font name), \code{size}, \code{color}, \code{weight}, \code{italic}, \code{spacing} (letter spacing),
\code{transform} (such as \code{"uppercase"}), and \code{align}, plus \code{line_height},
\code{margin_top}, \code{margin_bottom}, \code{padding_top}, and \code{padding_bottom}. Any length
takes a number, read as pixels, or a CSS string.
}

\section{Picking up the scale}{


\code{\link[=gt_color_ranks]{gt_color_ranks()}}, \code{\link[=gt_color_pills]{gt_color_pills()}} and \code{\link[=gt_percentile_bar]{gt_percentile_bar()}} record the
\code{columns}, \code{palette}, \code{domain}, \code{reverse} and \code{pal_type} they used on the
table. This function reads any of those the caller did not supply, so a legend
can be added with no arguments and cannot disagree with the cells it explains:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{gt(df) \%>\%
  gt_color_ranks(net, palette = "viridis::mako", domain = c(-10, 12)) \%>\%
  gt_legend_continuous()
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Anything passed explicitly wins over the recorded value. With several coloring
calls, the most recent one is the one recorded. The record is an attribute on
the table and survives the rest of a pipeline, apart from \code{\link[=gt_snake]{gt_snake()}}, which
rebuilds the table from its data; add the legend before snaking, or pass the
arguments directly.
}

